---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/literature/expert/comparative-literature">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the Literature track's summit — the lesson where every tier below becomes one
    discipline. World epics gave you the oldest texts; Close reading and Figures of speech
    trained the microscope; Literary theory supplied the schools; Modernism and postmodernism
    covered the great breaks; and The novel: a short history tracked the form that conquered
    the world. Comparative literature binds them: how texts travel between languages, what
    travels and what stays home, and who decides what the archive contains.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>compare the four world epics as one comparative problem — same questions, different technologies of transmission;</li>
    <li>distinguish influence from parallel by tracking specific, dated, unusual details;</li>
    <li>deploy the portable formalist toolkit — defamiliarisation, fabula/syuzhet, the poetic function, Propp's functions — across languages and genres;</li>
    <li>read a translation as an interpretation, name Venuti's two poles, and take a position on a real case;</li>
    <li>explain Damrosch's circulation definition of world literature, and why the edition is part of the text.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Comparative literature reads <strong>across borders: how texts travel, translate, influence
    and fail to travel</strong>. Born with Goethe's "world literature" prophecy (1827), it
    matured into the discipline that studies literature as a global system — epics answering
    each other across millennia, theories migrating between languages, translations acting as
    interpretations, and circulation itself deciding what survives. The unit's five topics are
    the discipline's spine: the great world epics as the shared archive, literary theory as its
    interpretive toolkit, the modernist and postmodernist breaks, translation as the mechanism
    of travel, and textual criticism (its neighbour chapter's full subject) as the craft that
    makes the archive readable at all.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="world literature, influence, translation, distant reading" widget="equationStep" config={compCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the panel runs the discipline's whole argument in four
    frames. Step 1 dismantles the national walls — Goethe's world-literature prophecy; step 2
    states the founding method, telling influence from convergent invention; step 3 makes
    translation the discipline's engine, every rendering a reading; step 4 introduces the
    modern controversy — Moretti's distant reading and the circulation question of which books
    cross borders. As you step through, watch one theme harden: what survives travel is never
    the whole text — circulation, translation and the edition all decide what "the work" is.
  </p>

  <h2>World epics: the shared deep archive</h2>
  <p>
    Four epics, four civilisations, one comparative discipline. Gilgamesh (Standard Babylonian
    version, ~1200 BCE) asks what a mortal should do with death; its flood tablet was
    recognised as parallel to Genesis the moment George Smith read it aloud in 1872 —
    comparative literature's founding sensation. The Iliad (~750–700 BCE) asks what honour is
    worth; Milman Parry's 1930s fieldwork showed its repeated phrases are oral-formulaic
    machinery — a technique for composing without writing, confirmed in living oral epics by
    Albert Lord. The Mahabharata (~100,000 couplets, about 1.8 million words, roughly ten times
    the Iliad and Odyssey combined) is an epic of dharma — duty in impossible situations — with
    the Bhagavad Gita at its centre. And the Sundiata epic (oral, Mali, commemorating the
    13th-century founder of the Mali empire) shows the archive's living form: performed by
    <em>jeliw</em> (griots), never fixed, its charter of social roles recited for seven
    centuries. Comparison begins here: same questions (mortality, duty, glory, founding),
    different technologies of transmission.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="pair each epic with its question and its transmission" widget="match" config={compEpicsMatch} />
  <p>
    <strong>Influence or parallel? The discipline's founding question.</strong> When the same
    motif appears in Baghdad and Berlin, did it travel or arise twice? The flood story is the
    showcase: the Atrahasis epic, Gilgamesh's tablet XI and Genesis share flood, warned
    survivor, vessel and birds sent to find land — details too specific for coincidence, and
    textual evidence (Mesopotamian versions centuries earlier) points to borrowing along the
    rivers of tradition. But the doomed-lovers motif, the trickster and the dragon-slaying
    hero appear too widely for single origin: convergent evolution of narrative — similar
    human situations generating similar stories. Distinguishing the two is comparative
    literature's core competence: track the specific, dated, unusual detail (borrowing
    inherits specifics; convergence reproduces generalities), and the circulation map
    follows.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "Homer was a single literate poet who wrote down the Iliad
    and Odyssey as we have them." Parry and Lord's evidence points the other way: the poems'
    repeated formulas and type-scenes are composition machinery for oral performance — each
    singing is a new realisation of a tradition, not a recitation of a fixed text — so the
    written Iliad we read is one crystallisation (probably sixth-century Athenian) of a
    performance practice centuries older. "Homer" may name a singular genius, a cumulative
    tradition, or both; the oral-formulaic finding is what turned the authorship question from
    biography into a researchable comparative problem.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="find the line the formulas refute" widget="spot-error" config={compHomerSpot} />

  <p><strong>So far:</strong> the shared archive is on the table, with its founding competence — telling borrowing (which inherits specifics) from convergent evolution (which reproduces generalities). The next section opens the toolkit: the schools of theory that make any text comparable in the first place.</p>

  <h2>Literary theory: formalism to postcolonialism</h2>
  <p>
    Theory is comparative literature's toolkit — each school a different question about the
    same text. Russian formalism (Shklovsky's "defamiliarisation", 1917: art makes the stone
    stony) asked what makes language literary; New Criticism read the self-contained poem;
    structuralism (Jakobson, Lévi-Strauss) sought the deep grammar of narrative; post-
    structuralism (Derrida) destabilised the sign; Marxism read literature as ideology's stage
    (Jameson: "always historicise"); feminism recovered the silenced and reread the canon
    (Gilbert and Gubar's madwoman; Showalter); reader-response moved meaning into the reading.
    Postcolonialism is comparative literature's conscience: Said's <em>Orientalism</em> (1978)
    showed how the West's representations of the East were instruments of rule; Spivak asked
    whether the subaltern can speak in the imperial languages at all; Achebe's English "carrying
    Igbo syntax" and Rushdie's "chutnified" English answered by remaking the coloniser's tongue
    from inside. The comparative skill: knowing which question a given reading is asking, and
    what it therefore cannot see.
  </p>
  <p>
    The formalist toolkit repays learning precisely because it travels between languages.
    Shklovsky's <em>ostranenie</em> (defamiliarisation) explains technique as managed
    difficulty: Tolstoy describing property as "sparking and hiving off" makes ownership
    strange again. The Russian formalists' <em>fabula</em>/<em>syuzhet</em> pair — the raw
    story versus its artistic arrangement — is still how narratology separates plot from
    chronology (a flashback is syuzhet machinery); Jakobson's poetic function (language
    drawn toward its own form — rhyme, parallelism) explains why verse translates worst;
    Propp's 31 functions across a hundred Russian fairy tales founded structural narratology
    and, transitively, every screenwriting manual. Each concept is a portable instrument:
    testable on an Igbo novel, a Noh play, a telenovela — which is exactly what comparative
    work needs.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="file each portable instrument by what it explains" widget="sort-buckets" config={compToolkitBuckets} />

  <p><strong>So far:</strong> the theory toolkit proved portable across languages and genres — testable on an Igbo novel, a Noh play, a telenovela. Now watch it work on the twentieth century's two great breaks, and discover that modernism was never only European.</p>

  <h2>Modernism and postmodernism: the two great breaks</h2>
  <p>
    Modernism (roughly 1910–1945) is the response to mechanised war, urban speed and the
    collapse of Victorian certainties: Joyce's <em>Ulysses</em> (1922) compressed Homer's epic
    day into Dublin's ordinary one; Eliot's <em>The Waste Land</em> (1922) assembled fragments
    from Sanskrit to Cockney as a broken culture's mosaic; Woolf and Faulkner dissolved
    narration into consciousness itself — stream of consciousness as formal principle.
    Postmodernism (from the 1960s) inherits the doubt and drops the grief: Borges's
    infinite library and Pynchon's paranoid systems make textuality itself the subject;
    metafiction confesses its own fictionality; pastiche replaces parody's sincerity; grand
    narratives lose credit (Lyotard's definition). The comparative pay-off: modernism is
    comprehensible as a worldwide reaction (its echoes run from Lagos to Tokyo), while
    postmodernism is comprehensible as a condition of global media — both breaks are arguments
    about what representation can do after disaster.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="drag the divider — reaction versus condition" widget="compare" config={compBreaksCompare} />
  <p>
    <strong>Modernism elsewhere, or: the break was polycentric.</strong> Reading modernism as a
    European crisis misplaces half the archive. São Paulo's Modern Art Week (1922) launched a
    Brazilian programme that culminated in Mário de Andrade's <em>Macunaíma</em> (1928), a
    "hero with no character" assembled from Amerindian myth and urban slang; the negritude
    poets (Césaire and Senghor, Paris in the 1930s) built a modernist French against the empire
    that owned the language; Kawabata and the New Sensationists rebuilt the Japanese novel
    around perception and native syntax; and the Harlem Renaissance ran the same experiment
    inside English. The comparative point: the break's techniques are portable, its causes are
    local (each modernism answers a different collapsed certainty), and translation carried the
    toolkit faster than the metropolitan centres noticed.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="true or false — the break was polycentric" widget="judge" config={compPolyJudge} />

  <p><strong>So far:</strong> both breaks turned out to be worldwide arguments about what representation can do after disaster. The next section reaches the discipline's engine: the translations that carry texts — and the languages that get remade receiving them.</p>

  <h2>Comparative literature and translation</h2>
  <p>
    Translation is the discipline's engine and its philosophical problem. Walter Benjamin
    ("The Task of the Translator", 1923) argued translation reveals what all language shares;
    the practical debate runs between Venuti's poles: <em>foreignisation</em> (keep the
    foreignness visible — the reader feels they are abroad) versus <em>domestication</em>
    (smooth into the target culture — the reader feels at home), and every translation takes a
    position. Homer's "wine-dark sea" (oinops pontos) has launched a hundred arguments: is it
    a colour, a sheen, a formula whose literal sense had faded? Edward Fitzgerald's
    <em>Rubaiyat of Omar Khayyam</em> (1859) — a loose re-creation that became an English
    classic while being, strictly, unfaithful — is the standing case that translation is
    interpretation, sometimes authorship. Damrosch's definition follows: world literature is
    not a canon of great books but a <em>mode of reading</em> — a work becomes world literature
    when it gains in circulation beyond its origin, and loses when it merely exits.
  </p>
  <p>
    Translation's history is also the history of languages being made. Luther's Bible
    (1522–34) and Tyndale's English New Testament (1526) did not just render scripture; they
    fixed vernacular prose style for centuries, pushing Luther's German and Tyndale's English
    (some 80% of the King James New Testament is Tyndale) into every church in Europe.
    Missionary linguistics continued the pattern worldwide — grammars and dictionaries of
    hundreds of languages written to translate scripture, a mixed legacy of documentation and
    imposition. The comparative lesson: translation flows downhill in prestige less often
    than it flows from power, and the receiving language is an active party — every
    translation deposits a layer in the borrower's literature (Victorian Japan's translations
    creating modern Japanese prose is the standard case).
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="pair each translation event with the language it made" widget="match" config={compBibleMatch} />
  <p>
    <strong>The largest translation programme on earth.</strong> Scripture translation remains
    the world's biggest literary project, and its statistics are the book trade's inverse: by
    the Wycliffe Global Alliance's 2024 count the full Bible exists in 756 languages, New
    Testaments in about 1,800, and translation work is under way in more than 3,200 — against
    roughly 7,000 living languages, leaving thousands still waiting. Every translation creates
    a literary tradition: hymnody, proverb collections, orthographies, the first printed book
    in hundreds of languages. Comparative literature's vernacular rise — Luther's German,
    Tyndale's English, the missionary grammarians who wrote the first grammars of hundreds of
    languages in order to translate — is one story told at two scales: documentation and
    imposition arriving in the same parcel.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="call the count — Bibles in languages" widget="estimate" config={compScriptureEstimate} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "world literature is the canon of the world's best books."
    Damrosch's definition is deliberately different: it names a <em>mode of circulation and
    reading</em>, not a shelf. A work is world literature when it gains from leaving home —
    and the same book can be world literature in Stockholm and merely exported in Lagos,
    depending on the reading. Canons are the field's data, not its definition.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "translation is a mechanical word-swap, so a 'literal'
    translation is the most faithful one." Languages partition experience differently — tense,
    formality, kinship, sound — so a word-for-word rendering is usually the LEAST faithful to
    the work's effect. Every translation is a structured set of interpretations; the honest
    question is which interpretation, and for which readers.
  </p>

  <h2>Textual criticism and scholarly editions</h2>
  <p>
    Circulation requires surviving copies, and copies carry errors — which is why comparative
    literature leans on its craft neighbour. The epics themselves are reconstructions: Gilgamesh
    is edited from fragmentary tablets across a millennium of copies; the Mahabharata's
    celebrated Critical Edition (Bhandarkar Oriental Research Institute, Pune, 1919–1966)
    collated over a thousand manuscripts to approximate the earliest recoverable text; the
    Iliad rests on medieval manuscripts (the Venetus A of ~1000 CE is the most famous) plus
    the Alexandrian scholars' ancient editorial labours (Zenodotus to Aristarchus, 3rd–2nd
    centuries BCE — history's first textual critics). Sundiata has no text at all in the
    classical sense: its "editions" are transcriptions of performances, each one already an
    interpretation. A scholarly edition is therefore an argument — about which variants are
    errors, which are revisions, and which version deserves the name of the work — and reading
    world literature knowingly means reading the edition's apparatus as part of the text.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="find the line the editions contradict" widget="spot-error" config={compTextSpot} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>The flood narrative appears in Gilgamesh's tablet XI and Genesis. Why do comparatists read this as borrowing, while the doomed-lovers motif's worldwide spread is read as convergent evolution?</li>
    <li>A translator rendering Homer's "wine-dark sea" must choose between keeping the strangeness and smoothing it for the target reader. Name Venuti's two poles, and say why Fitzgerald's Rubaiyat is the standing hard case.</li>
    <li>Why does Damrosch make world literature a mode of reading rather than a canon — and what does that definition predict about the same book in different markets?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Because the flood versions share specific, dated, unusual
    details — warned survivor, vessel, birds sent to find land — with the Mesopotamian texts
    centuries earlier: borrowing inherits specifics. Doomed lovers, tricksters and
    dragon-slayers appear too widely for a single origin and reproduce only generalities —
    similar human situations generating similar stories. The working rule: track the specific,
    dated, unusual detail, and the circulation map follows. (2) Foreignisation (keep the
    foreignness visible — the reader feels they are abroad) versus domestication (smooth into
    the target culture — the reader feels at home). Fitzgerald's 1859 Rubaiyat is the hard
    case because a loose re-creation — strictly unfaithful — became an English classic,
    proving that translation can be interpretation, even authorship. (3) Because on Damrosch's
    account a work becomes world literature when it gains in circulation beyond its origin —
    a property of reception, not of a shelf. The prediction: the same book can be world
    literature in Stockholm and merely exported in Lagos — the difference is the reading, not
    the text.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Comparative literature reads across borders: how texts travel, translate, influence and fail to travel.</li>
    <li>The four world epics are one comparative problem — same questions (mortality, duty, glory, founding), different transmission technologies.</li>
    <li>Borrowing inherits specifics; convergence reproduces generalities — the founding test for influence.</li>
    <li>The formalist toolkit travels: defamiliarisation, fabula/syuzhet, Jakobson's poetic function, Propp's functions.</li>
    <li>Modernism was polycentric; postmodernism is a condition of global media — both breaks argue what representation can do after disaster.</li>
    <li>Translation is interpretation: foreignisation versus domestication, and translations that make languages (Luther, Tyndale).</li>
    <li>World literature is a mode of circulation and reading (Damrosch) — and every text survives as an edition, an argument about which variants are the work.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The other expert lesson in Literature, Textual criticism and editing, takes this lesson's
    craft neighbour to full depth: stemma, variants and the making of scholarly editions. To
    strengthen the toolkit this lesson borrowed, return to the advanced lessons Literary
    theory and Modernism and postmodernism; and to see the archive's oldest layer again,
    World epics is where this track's comparative thread began.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Distant reading, seriously.</strong> Franco Moretti's programme — quantitative
    patterns across thousands of texts (genre rise-and-fall curves, the seven basic plot shapes
    his Stanford lab extracted from thousands of plots) — divides the field: the patterns are
    real and close reading could never find them; but quantification detaches language from
    meaning, and its corpora (digitised, mostly Anglophone, mostly books) are not literature.
    The current synthesis is "macroanalysis" plus close reading of the outliers the numbers
    flag — a division of labour, argued as fiercely as any method debate in the social
    sciences.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>World-systems literature.</strong> The Pascale Casanova / Moretti debate on how
    literary prestige works: Paris as "Greenwich meridian of literature", languages as
    core and periphery, translation as the visible trace of power. Postcolonial writers'
    strategies (vernacularising the imperial language, self-translation, staying local and
    letting translation come to you) are the practical rebuttals — and the current global
    novel (Ferrante's dialect-inflected Italian to Ethiopian-American autofiction) is the live
    dataset.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Does machine translation make world literature universal —
    or flatten the very foreignness the discipline exists to preserve? Can distant reading of
    non-European corpora rebalance the field's Anglophone tilt? And what happens to influence
    studies when generative models can produce Homer-inflected verse on demand — is the
    author-function itself now an edition? The discipline's next arguments are already drafted.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Goethe's conversations with Eckermann (1827); Damrosch, What Is World Literature? (2003); Moretti, Graphs, Maps, Trees (2005); Venuti, The Translator's Invisibility. Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>
